% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 6, section 6.2, Examples 2 and 3 (restrictive vs
%         non-restrictive relative clauses in CL form; rewriting an
%         ambiguous relative clause).
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book observes that English relative clauses ("a person who ...") can be
% restrictive (they qualify the condition of a rule) or non-restrictive (they
% add a further conclusion). In CL/LE both readings become plain Head-if-Body
% conditionals; the relative clause "who is a British citizen" becomes the
% body condition. We render four such rules:
%   - deprivation of citizenship (restrictive: only those who lied),
%   - entitlement to vote in EU elections,
%   - being an EU citizen,
%   - owing loyalty to the Crown (further conclusion for those with abode).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* is a British citizen.
*a person* may be deprived of British citizenship.
*a person* obtains citizenship by providing false information.
*a person* is entitled to vote in EU elections.
*a person* is an EU citizen.
*a person* owes loyalty to the Crown.
*a person* has the right of abode in the UK.

the knowledge base relative clauses includes:

% Restrictive: only a citizen who lied may be deprived of citizenship.
a person may be deprived of British citizenship
    if the person is a British citizen
    and the person obtains citizenship by providing false information.

% A British citizen is entitled to vote in EU elections.
a person is entitled to vote in EU elections
    if the person is a British citizen.

% A British citizen is an EU citizen.
a person is an EU citizen
    if the person is a British citizen.

% A British citizen who has the right of abode owes loyalty to the Crown.
a person owes loyalty to the Crown
    if the person is a British citizen
    and the person has the right of abode in the UK.

scenario citizens is:
    alice is a British citizen.
    bob is a British citizen.
    alice obtains citizenship by providing false information.
    bob has the right of abode in the UK.
    deprivation expects answers ["alice may be deprived of British citizenship"].
    voting expects answers ["alice is entitled to vote in EU elections", "bob is entitled to vote in EU elections"].
    eu_citizens expects answers ["alice is an EU citizen", "bob is an EU citizen"].
    loyalty expects answers ["bob owes loyalty to the Crown"].

query deprivation is:
    which person may be deprived of British citizenship.

query voting is:
    which person is entitled to vote in EU elections.

query eu_citizens is:
    which person is an EU citizen.

query loyalty is:
    which person owes loyalty to the Crown.
